# Preflight (Base Styles) 🛠️

Built on top of **modern-normalize**, Preflight is a set of base styles designed to smooth out cross-browser inconsistencies.

## Key Resets Applied by Preflight
1. **Margin Resets**: All margins (`body`, `h1`-`h6`, `p`, `ul`, etc.) are removed (`margin: 0`).
2. **Unstyled Headings**: `h1` through `h6` are unstyled by default, matching standard text size and font weight.
3. **Lists are Unstyled**: `<ul>` and `<ol>` have no list-style markers or padding.
4. **Images are Block-Level**: `img`, `svg`, `video`, and `canvas` are `display: block` and `max-width: 100%`.
5. **Borders Reset**: Default border styles are `border: 0 solid #e5e7eb`.

## Adding Custom Base Styles
You can augment Preflight using the `@layer base` directive:

```css_not
@tailwind base;
@tailwind components;
@tailwind utilities;

@layer base {
  h1 {
    @apply text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900;
  }
  a {
    @apply text-blue-600 hover:underline;
  }
}
```

# Dark Mode in Tailwind CSS 🌙

Tailwind provides a first-class `dark:` variant that makes it effortless to style your site differently when dark mode is enabled.

## Two Strategies for Dark Mode

### Strategy 1: Media Query (`media` - default)
Automatically respects the user's operating system preference (`prefers-color-scheme: dark`).

```javascript_not
// tailwind.config.js
module.exports = {
  darkMode: 'media', // default
};
```

### Strategy 2: Class Strategy (`class` / `selector`)
Allows manual toggling via JavaScript by adding or removing the `dark` class on the `<html>` or `<body>` element:

```javascript_not
// tailwind.config.js
module.exports = {
  darkMode: 'class', // or 'selector' in Tailwind v3.4+
};
```

# Dark Mode Implementation & JS Toggle 🌓

Here is how you apply dark variants in markup and wire up a persistent theme switcher.

## Applying `dark:` Classes

```html_not
<div class="bg-white text-slate-900 dark:bg-slate-900 dark:text-white p-8 rounded-2xl shadow-xl transition-colors">
  <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold">Dark Mode Ready</h2>
  <p class="text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 mt-2">
    This card seamlessly shifts between light and dark modes.
  </p>
  <button id="theme-toggle" class="mt-4 px-4 py-2 bg-indigo-600 dark:bg-indigo-500 text-white rounded-lg">
    Toggle Theme
  </button>
</div>
```

## JavaScript Toggle Logic

```javascript_not
const toggleBtn = document.getElementById('theme-toggle');
toggleBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
  if (document.documentElement.classList.contains('dark')) {
    document.documentElement.classList.remove('dark');
    localStorage.theme = 'light';
  } else {
    document.documentElement.classList.add('dark');
    localStorage.theme = 'dark';
  }
});
```

# Understanding tailwind.config.js ⚙️

The `tailwind.config.js` file is the central command center for customizing your design system.

```javascript_not
/** @type {import('tailwindcss').Config} */
module.exports = {
  content: [
    './src/**/*.{html,js,jsx,ts,tsx}',
    './public/index.html'
  ],
  darkMode: 'class',
  theme: {
    extend: {
      colors: {
        primary: '#3b82f6',
      },
      fontFamily: {
        heading: ['Inter', 'sans-serif'],
      },
    },
  },
  plugins: [],
};
```

## The `content` Array
The `content` array defines which files Tailwind scans for class names. If a file is omitted, its Tailwind classes will **not** be included in your generated CSS!

# Core CSS Directives: @tailwind 📜

To inject Tailwind's generated styles into your CSS bundle, include the three main directives in your entry CSS file (`src/input.css`):

```css_not
/* 1. Base Preflight styles & element defaults */
@tailwind base;

/* 2. Component classes registered via plugins or @layer */
@tailwind components;

/* 3. All utility classes and variants (hover, responsive, dark) */
@tailwind utilities;
```

## Why Order Matters
- `base` comes first so utilities can override base element styles.
- `components` comes second so utilities can override component presets.
- `utilities` comes last ensuring utilities always win specificity conflicts.

# Organizing Code with @layer 📂

When you need custom CSS rules, wrap them in `@layer` so Tailwind injects them into the correct specificity bucket:

```css_not
@tailwind base;
@tailwind components;
@tailwind utilities;

@layer components {
  .btn-primary {
    padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
    border-radius: 0.375rem;
    background-color: #4f46e5;
    color: #ffffff;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
}

@layer utilities {
  .content-auto {
    content-visibility: auto;
  }
}
```

## Benefit of `@layer`
Any custom CSS inside `@layer` will automatically support state variants like `hover:btn-primary` and responsive prefixes like `md:btn-primary`!

# The @apply Directive: Power & Pitfalls ⚠️

The `@apply` directive lets you inline existing utility classes into custom CSS rules.

```css_not
@layer components {
  .badge {
    @apply inline-flex items-center px-2.5 py-0.5 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold bg-blue-100 text-blue-800;
  }
}
```

## When to Use `@apply`
- ✅ Small, highly repetitive third-party widget overrides.
- ✅ When you cannot use component templates (e.g., pure Markdown content).

## When to Avoid `@apply`
- ❌ Recreating traditional monolithic stylesheets.
- ❌ Component reuse in React/Vue/Svelte (use framework components instead).
- ❌ Causes CSS bundle bloat if overused.

# Crucial Gotcha: Dynamic Class Names 💥

Tailwind's JIT scanner uses **raw string extraction** (regular expressions). It does NOT execute JavaScript!

## The Broken Pattern ❌

```javascript_not
// BROKEN: Tailwind will NEVER see 'bg-red-500' or 'bg-blue-500'!
const color = 'red';
const badge = `<div class="bg-${color}-500">Badge</div>`;
```

## The Correct Pattern ✅

Always map complete, unbroken class names:

```javascript_not
// CORRECT: Full strings are visible to the scanner
const colorMap = {
  red: 'bg-red-500 text-red-100',
  blue: 'bg-blue-500 text-blue-100',
  green: 'bg-green-500 text-green-100',
};

const badge = `<div class="${colorMap[color]}">Badge</div>`;
```
